---
<ArticleLayout
  title="How to Calculate BMI – Formula, Chart & Complete Guide 2026 | Helthvibe"
  description="Learn the exact BMI formula in metric and imperial, see the official WHO chart, and understand each category. Free calculator included."
  h1="How to Calculate BMI — Formula, Chart & Complete Guide"
  excerpt="The exact BMI formula, the WHO adult chart, and step-by-step worked examples in both metric and imperial units."
  datePublished="2026-09-27"
  dateModified="2026-09-27"
  category="Guide"
  readTime="8 min"
  showCalculator={true}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <p>
    Body Mass Index (BMI) is the most widely used screening tool for weight categories. It is a single number that puts your
    height and weight into a clinical context — underweight, healthy, overweight, or obese. The math behind it is simple, but
    knowing what the number actually means (and what it doesn't) takes a bit more.
  </p>

  <p>
    This guide covers the exact formula in metric and imperial, two worked examples, the official WHO adult chart, and the
    places where BMI breaks down. Use the calculator above to get your number — or read on to do it by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>The metric BMI formula</h2>
  <p>
    The original BMI formula was developed in the 19th century by the Belgian mathematician Adolphe Quetelet and is sometimes
    called the Quetelet index. In metric units, it is:
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;font-size:1.3rem;font-weight:600;padding:var(--space-4);background:var(--color-bg-soft);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
    BMI = weight (kg) ÷ height (m)²
  </p>

  <p>The two steps are:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Square your height in meters (multiply it by itself).</li>
    <li>Divide your weight in kilograms by that number.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Worked example — metric</h3>
  <p>Imagine someone who is 1.75 m tall and weighs 70 kg.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Height squared:</strong> 1.75 × 1.75 = 3.0625</li>
    <li><strong>BMI:</strong> 70 ÷ 3.0625 = <strong>22.9</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>22.9 falls into the WHO "healthy range" (18.5–24.9).</p>

  <h2>The imperial BMI formula</h2>
  <p>
    In the United States, height is measured in inches and weight in pounds. The imperial formula uses a conversion constant
    (703) so the resulting number matches the metric version:
  </p>

  <p style="text-align:center;font-size:1.3rem;font-weight:600;padding:var(--space-4);background:var(--color-bg-soft);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
    BMI = (weight (lb) × 703) ÷ height (in)²
  </p>

  <p>The two steps are:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Square your height in inches.</li>
    <li>Multiply your weight in pounds by 703, then divide by that number.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Tip:</strong> Don't confuse yourself with feet vs. inches. Convert your height to total inches first
    (5'7" = 67 inches, 6'2" = 74 inches).
  </div>

  <h3>Worked example — imperial</h3>
  <p>Imagine someone who is 5'7" (67 in) tall and weighs 154 lb.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Height in inches squared:</strong> 67 × 67 = 4,489</li>
    <li><strong>Weight × 703:</strong> 154 × 703 = 108,262</li>
    <li><strong>BMI:</strong> 108,262 ÷ 4,489 = <strong>24.1</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>24.1 is at the high end of the WHO healthy range.</p>

  <h2>The official WHO adult BMI chart</h2>
  <p>
    The World Health Organization defines four adult BMI categories using these cutoffs:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>WHO adult BMI classification (1995, in widespread use through 2026)</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>BMI</th><th>Category</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 18.5</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>18.5 – 24.9</td><td>Healthy range</td></tr>
      <tr><td>25.0 – 29.9</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30.0 and above</td><td>Obese</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    "Obese" is often broken into three sub-classes: Class I (30–34.9), Class II (35–39.9), and Class III (≥40), sometimes
    called "severe" or "morbid" obesity. Higher classes carry higher health risks. See our
    [categories guide](/articles/bmi-categories-explained) for the full breakdown.
  </p>

  <h2>Asian-adjusted thresholds</h2>
  <p>
    Research shows that Asian populations tend to develop weight-related health complications at lower BMIs than
    European-origin populations. The WHO has issued separate cutoffs that lower the "healthy" band and lower the
    "overweight" and "obese" cutoffs:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>WHO Asian-adjusted BMI classification</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>BMI</th><th>Category</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 18.5</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>18.5 – 22.9</td><td>Healthy range</td></tr>
      <tr><td>23.0 – 27.4</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>27.5 and above</td><td>Obese</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    If you have South, East, or Southeast Asian heritage, consider using our
    [Asian BMI calculator](/calculators/bmi-asian) — it has a built-in toggle so you can see both.
  </p>

  <h2>How to find your healthy weight range from BMI</h2>
  <p>
    A useful next step after calculating your BMI is to find the weight range that puts your BMI in the healthy band
    (18.5–24.9). The math is:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lower bound:</strong> 18.5 × (your height in meters)²</li>
    <li><strong>Upper bound:</strong> 24.9 × (your height in meters)²</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For someone who is 1.75 m tall:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Lower: 18.5 × 3.0625 = <strong>56.7 kg</strong> (about 125 lb)</li>
    <li>Upper: 24.9 × 3.0625 = <strong>76.3 kg</strong> (about 168 lb)</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That gives a healthy weight range of roughly <strong>56.7–76.3 kg (125–168 lb)</strong> for someone 1.75 m / 5'9"
    tall. The calculator above does this for you automatically and shows it in your preferred units.
  </p>

  <h2>Where BMI breaks down</h2>
  <p>
    BMI is useful as a quick screening tool, but it has real limitations. It does not distinguish muscle from fat, and it
    does not account for bone density, body shape, age, ethnicity, or sex-based differences in body composition.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--warning">
    <strong>BMI is misleading for these groups:</strong>
    Athletes and very muscular individuals (BMI may overestimate "overweight"), the elderly (who tend to lose muscle mass),
    pregnant or breastfeeding women, and certain ethnic populations where standard cutoffs misclassify cardiometabolic risk.
  </div>

  <p>
    For these groups, body-fat percentage, waist-to-height ratio, or a clinical assessment by a healthcare provider gives a
    more accurate picture. See our [accuracy guide](/articles/is-bmi-accurate-in-2026) for the full breakdown.
  </p>

  <h2>BMI vs. body-fat percentage</h2>
  <p>
    Body-fat percentage measures what BMI cannot: how much of your weight is actually fat versus muscle, bone, and water.
    A "healthy" body-fat percentage depends on age and sex:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>Healthy body-fat percentage ranges (American Council on Exercise)</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Group</th><th>Essential fat</th><th>Athletes</th><th>Healthy</th><th>Acceptable</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Men</td><td>2–5%</td><td>6–13%</td><td>14–17%</td><td>18–24%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Women</td><td>10–13%</td><td>14–20%</td><td>21–24%</td><td>25–31%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    You can measure body-fat percentage with calipers, bioelectrical impedance scales (BIA), DEXA scans, or hydrostatic
    weighing. DEXA is the clinical gold standard. Our [BMI vs. body-fat guide](/articles/bmi-vs-body-fat-percentage)
    compares the two in detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Measuring yourself at the wrong time of day.</strong> Weight fluctuates 1–2 kg from morning to evening due to food, water, and digestion.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the wrong height.</strong> Self-reported height is often off by 1–2 cm; use a stadiometer if possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating BMI as a verdict.</strong> It's a screening signal — not a diagnosis. Talk to a clinician for personalized guidance.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring waist circumference.</strong> For waist-to-height ratio, a number above 0.5 signals elevated risk regardless of BMI.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to do with your number</h2>
  <p>
    Once you have your BMI, the right next step depends on which band you fall into. See our
    [categories guide](/articles/bmi-categories-explained) for action steps per band, or our
    [action guide](/articles/from-bmi-to-action) for a quick decision tree.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> BMI is a screening tool, not a diagnosis. Always consult a qualified healthcare
    professional for personal medical decisions.
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
